1
00:00:00,700 --> 00:00:07,341
A rock contains many pores. Why can water enter on the left, yet fail to reach the right?

2
00:00:07,741 --> 00:00:14,788
Look closer: some pores connect to nearby pores, but then meet a wall.

3
00:00:15,188 --> 00:00:19,902
The number of pores matters. So does whether they form a connected route.

4
00:00:20,302 --> 00:00:25,329
A reproducible random experiment will help us understand the percolation threshold.

5
00:00:26,729 --> 00:00:34,670
Simplify the rock into a grid: blue sites are open; dark sites are blocked.

6
00:00:35,070 --> 00:00:44,451
Each site opens independently with probability p. It is a model parameter, not the exact open fraction in one trial.

7
00:00:44,851 --> 00:00:51,806
Open sites connect only through shared edges: up, down, left or right. Diagonal contact does not count.

8
00:00:52,206 --> 00:00:58,939
Explore open sites from the left boundary. Reaching the right boundary means a crossing.

9
00:00:59,339 --> 00:01:06,433
This is a connectivity model. The water shows reachability, not physical flow speed.

10
00:01:07,833 --> 00:01:12,547
At low opening probability, most open sites form small, scattered islands.

11
00:01:12,947 --> 00:01:16,894
Each connected group of open sites is called a cluster.

12
00:01:17,294 --> 00:01:22,089
As opening probability increases, new sites can join existing clusters.

13
00:01:22,489 --> 00:01:30,384
A larger cluster need not span the grid. Only the gold region is reachable from the inlet.

14
00:01:30,784 --> 00:01:34,685
Blue regions are open, but may remain disconnected from the inlet.

15
00:01:36,085 --> 00:01:42,819
Keep the same random sample, and raise p from 58 percent to 60 percent.

16
00:01:43,219 --> 00:01:49,360
Each site has a preassigned random number, so increasing p only adds open sites.

17
00:01:49,760 --> 00:01:56,088
Watch: newly opened sites join clusters, creating a route across the grid.

18
00:01:56,488 --> 00:02:02,676
Slow it down: a few local connections change connectivity across the whole grid.

19
00:02:03,076 --> 00:02:09,043
This sample was selected for illustration. Its first-crossing value is not the theoretical threshold.

20
00:02:10,443 --> 00:02:15,877
A different random grid can give a different result at the same p.

21
00:02:16,277 --> 00:02:22,152
We run one thousand independent trials for each probability on a 128 by 128 grid.

22
00:02:22,552 --> 00:02:29,692
At p = 58%, 187 trials cross; at p = 60%, 700 trials cross.

23
00:02:30,092 --> 00:02:35,606
These are the results of this simulation. The whiskers show 95% confidence intervals.

24
00:02:36,006 --> 00:02:42,195
The difference can be substantial, but failure in a finite grid does not mean mathematical impossibility.

25
00:02:43,595 --> 00:02:52,209
The horizontal axis is p; the vertical axis is crossing probability. Each point comes from 500 trials.

26
00:02:52,609 --> 00:02:58,670
Compare three grid sizes: 32, 64 and 128 sites per side.

27
00:02:59,070 --> 00:03:04,770
Larger grids usually show a sharper transition near criticality: a finite-size effect.

28
00:03:05,170 --> 00:03:13,111
For independent site percolation on an infinite square lattice with four neighbors, the threshold is about 59.27%.

29
00:03:13,511 --> 00:03:19,525
This number belongs to a specific model. It is not a universal constant for materials and networks.

30
00:03:20,925 --> 00:03:28,913
Replace pores with conducting particles in an insulating material. Connectivity matters again.

31
00:03:29,313 --> 00:03:33,075
Isolated particles cannot form a conducting route across the sample.

32
00:03:33,475 --> 00:03:38,815
When the contact network connects both sides, a route becomes available for current.

33
00:03:39,215 --> 00:03:47,563
Particle shape, contact rules and spatial dimension affect the threshold. This is a simplified illustration.

34
00:03:48,963 --> 00:03:54,431
Remember three ideas: opening probability, clusters and crossings.

35
00:03:54,831 --> 00:04:01,159
Percolation studies how local random connections create system-wide routes.

36
00:04:01,559 --> 00:04:09,454
Near criticality, small increases can strongly change connectivity. Repeated trials are still needed.

37
00:04:09,854 --> 00:04:17,261
It can inspire thinking about connections, but cannot prove that 58% effort equals no effort.

38
00:04:17,661 --> 00:04:22,688
Beyond quantity, look at connectivity. Beyond one result, look at probability.
